1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,000
Tan Kim Seng and the
Fountain Nobody Can Explain

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,918
At Esplanade Park, an ornate
Victorian iron fountain has stood

3
00:00:07,998 --> 00:00:11,916
for well over a century, its
three tiers ringed with classical

4
00:00:11,996 --> 00:00:16,431
figures and its basin fed by four
spouts shaped like Poseidon's face —

5
00:00:16,511 --> 00:00:19,913
or it would be fed, except
several of those spouts had

6
00:00:19,993 --> 00:00:23,718
been sealed shut for years,
quietly turning a monument to a

7
00:00:23,798 --> 00:00:27,925
water donation into a fountain
that couldn't actually run water.

8
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:32,175
Thousands of joggers and
tourists pass it every week.

9
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:35,434
Almost none stop to read
the inscription that

10
00:00:35,514 --> 00:00:39,150
explains why it's there,
or who Tan Kim Seng was.

11
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:43,292
Tan Kim Seng was born in
1805 in Dutch Malacca,

12
00:00:43,372 --> 00:00:47,325
a Straits-born Peranakan
of Hokkien descent.

13
00:00:47,325 --> 00:00:51,996
He founded the trading house Kim
Seng & Company in 1840 and built

14
00:00:52,076 --> 00:00:56,967
one of the largest fortunes in early
Singapore — by the time he died

15
00:00:57,047 --> 00:01:01,725
he was estimated to hold roughly
half the island's private land.

16
00:01:01,725 --> 00:01:06,048
His standing in both the Chinese
and colonial worlds made him

17
00:01:06,128 --> 00:01:10,451
one of the few merchants the
government actually listened to:

18
00:01:10,531 --> 00:01:15,287
he was appointed a Justice of the
Peace in 1850, and in 1857 became

19
00:01:15,367 --> 00:01:19,625
the first Asian member of
Singapore's Municipal Commission.

20
00:01:19,625 --> 00:01:24,229
Three years earlier, in May 1854,
he'd used exactly that standing

21
00:01:24,309 --> 00:01:29,130
to help end the Hokkien-Teochew
riots — ten days of fighting between

22
00:01:29,210 --> 00:01:33,310
rival secret societies, sparked
by a dispute so small it's

23
00:01:33,390 --> 00:01:38,499
remembered as the "Five Catties of
Rice Riots," that killed an estimated

24
00:01:38,579 --> 00:01:42,975
500 people and destroyed some
300 homes before it burned out.

25
00:01:42,975 --> 00:01:46,648
He also founded Chong Wen
Ge, one of Singapore's

26
00:01:46,728 --> 00:01:50,870
earliest Chinese schools,
in 1849, and gave generously

27
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:55,014
to Tan Tock Seng Hospital,
a cause the philanthropist

28
00:01:55,094 --> 00:01:58,925
Tan Tock Seng had championed
a decade before him.

29
00:01:58,925 --> 00:02:02,950
None of that is what put his
name on a fountain, though.

30
00:02:02,950 --> 00:02:07,189
On the 18th of November 1857,
Tan offered the government

31
00:02:07,269 --> 00:02:11,200
$13,000 toward Singapore's
first proper waterworks —

32
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:15,751
piped water carried down from
a reservoir near Bukit Timah,

33
00:02:15,831 --> 00:02:20,150
on condition that it stay
free to the public once built.

34
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:23,402
The government took the
money and then spent the next

35
00:02:23,482 --> 00:02:26,625
two decades bungling what
it was meant to pay for.

36
00:02:26,625 --> 00:02:30,476
Contractors laid earthenware
pipes at Kandang Kerbau that

37
00:02:30,556 --> 00:02:34,201
turned out to be useless for
the job; sections of pipe

38
00:02:34,281 --> 00:02:38,064
sat unused at the harbour;
at one point an engineer drew

39
00:02:38,144 --> 00:02:42,075
up a scheme that would have
required water to run uphill.

40
00:02:42,075 --> 00:02:47,757
The waterworks weren't finished until
1877, opening the following year —

41
00:02:47,837 --> 00:02:51,998
thirteen years after Tan
had died in Malacca in 1864,

42
00:02:52,078 --> 00:02:57,600
without ever seeing a drop of the
water his gift was meant to supply.

43
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:01,299
The Municipal Commissioners
built the fountain

44
00:03:01,379 --> 00:03:05,075
in 1882 partly to make
amends for the fiasco.

45
00:03:05,075 --> 00:03:09,455
A Victorian, three-tiered,
cast-iron structure by the English

46
00:03:09,535 --> 00:03:13,622
firm Andrew Handyside & Co.,
it was unveiled at Fullerton

47
00:03:13,702 --> 00:03:18,228
Square on the 18th of May 1882,
its inscription crediting Tan's

48
00:03:18,308 --> 00:03:22,475
donation "towards the cost of
the Singapore Water Works."

49
00:03:22,475 --> 00:03:26,970
It stayed there until Fullerton
Square itself was redeveloped

50
00:03:27,050 --> 00:03:30,950
in the 1920s to make way
for the Fullerton Building.

51
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:34,463
A Straits Budget report
from May 1929 describes the

52
00:03:34,543 --> 00:03:38,408
fountain's removal as still
under way after "controversy

53
00:03:38,488 --> 00:03:42,141
lasting nearly eight years"
over where it should go —

54
00:03:42,221 --> 00:03:46,016
it was eventually dismantled
and rebuilt at its current

55
00:03:46,096 --> 00:03:49,900
site in Esplanade Park, where
it has stood ever since.

56
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:54,552
It was gazetted a national
monument in 2010, and has been

57
00:03:54,632 --> 00:03:59,698
substantially restored twice:
once in 1994, for $1.12 million,

58
00:03:59,778 --> 00:04:02,853
and again as recently as 2023 to 2024,

59
00:04:02,933 --> 00:04:08,165
in what conservators called the
most extensive intervention yet,

60
00:04:08,245 --> 00:04:13,475
with 257 separate components
taken apart, cleaned and recoated.

61
00:04:13,475 --> 00:04:18,100
Tan Kim Seng's own philanthropy
didn't end with him, either.

62
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:21,302
His son, Tan Beng Swee,
carried on the family's

63
00:04:21,382 --> 00:04:24,653
giving in Malacca, funding
a bridge and a burial

64
00:04:24,733 --> 00:04:27,875
ground that still bear
the family name there.

65
00:04:27,875 --> 00:04:31,295
His grandson, Tan Jiak
Kim, went further still:

66
00:04:31,375 --> 00:04:36,434
in October 1904 he launched the
fundraising campaign that founded the

67
00:04:36,514 --> 00:04:39,711
King Edward the Seventh
College of Medicine,

68
00:04:39,791 --> 00:04:44,924
personally donating $12,000 toward
what would eventually grow into the

69
00:04:45,004 --> 00:04:49,175
medical faculty of the National
University of Singapore.

70
00:04:49,175 --> 00:04:52,745
Three generations of one
family — a water donation the

71
00:04:52,825 --> 00:04:57,342
government couldn't get right, a
bridge in Malacca, and, eventually,

72
00:04:57,422 --> 00:05:00,857
a medical school — and
almost none of it is what the

73
00:05:00,937 --> 00:05:04,925
fountain most people photograph
is actually remembered for.

74
00:05:04,925 --> 00:05:08,709
Why it matters today: the
fountain most people photograph

75
00:05:08,789 --> 00:05:11,895
on a walk through Esplanade
Park doesn't really

76
00:05:11,975 --> 00:05:15,691
commemorate a gift of water
— it commemorates a colonial

77
00:05:15,771 --> 00:05:19,025
government's twenty-year
failure to deliver one.

78
00:05:19,025 --> 00:05:23,770
The lasting result of Tan Kim Seng's
money wasn't the waterworks at all;

79
00:05:23,850 --> 00:05:28,125
it was three generations of a
family that kept building the civic

80
00:05:28,205 --> 00:05:32,749
institutions the government wouldn't
fund on its own, from a hospital

81
00:05:32,829 --> 00:05:37,050
and a school to, eventually,
Singapore's first medical college.

82
00:05:37,050 --> 00:05:41,486
Actual water security took another
century and a half to solve —

83
00:05:41,566 --> 00:05:46,575
that's the story Marina Barrage and
Linggiu Reservoir finished telling.
